CARDS = [
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Your Node service's p99 latency doubled overnight. CPU is at 40%. Where do you look?", "40% average CPU with bad p99 is the signature of stalls, not saturation. Three candidates in order: CPU throttling — check <code>nr_throttled</code> in the cgroup's <code>cpu.stat</code>, because a container can burn its quota in the first 30 ms of each 100 ms period and sleep for 70; a blocked event loop — check the event loop delay histogram and the accept queue with <code>ss -lnt</code>; and GC pauses — check <code>--trace-gc</code> or a PerformanceObserver on gc entries."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "A container is being OOMKilled but the heap graph is flat. What is happening?", "Something outside the V8 heap is consuming the cgroup budget. Three usual causes: Buffers or a native addon leaking, which shows in <code>external</code> and RSS but not the heap; page cache from the container's own file writes, which is charged to the cgroup — check the anon versus file split in <code>memory.stat</code>; or a memory-medium <code>emptyDir</code>, which is tmpfs and counts against the limit. If it is the second, log to stdout instead of a file."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Every deploy produces a burst of 502s. Why?", "A race between two things that happen concurrently on pod deletion: the kubelet sends SIGTERM, and the endpoints controller removes the pod from the Service — but that removal has to propagate to kube-proxy on every node. If you stop accepting connections the instant you get SIGTERM, traffic still being routed to you is refused. The fix is to fail readiness first, keep serving for a few seconds, and only then close — or a preStop hook with a sleep."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "A process will not die, even with kill -9. What is going on?", "It is in D state — uninterruptible sleep — inside the kernel at a point where waking it would corrupt state, almost always partway through a disk or network filesystem operation. SIGKILL is queued and delivered when it leaves D, and if the underlying device never responds it never leaves. <code>cat /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stack</code> tells you which kernel operation. The only remedies are fixing the device or rebooting."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Your service starts failing with EMFILE: too many open files. How do you find the cause?", "<code>ls -l /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd</code> lists every descriptor with its target — sockets, pipes, paths — and <code>wc -l</code> against <code>grep 'Max open files' /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/limits</code> confirms you are at the ceiling. Almost always it is a leak rather than real concurrency: HTTP responses never consumed, streams never closed, connections without a keep-alive timeout. Raise the limit only after you have looked, because a leak will exhaust any limit."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Requests to one service intermittently fail across every workload on one node, and all the applications are healthy. What would you check?", "The conntrack table. NAT and stateful firewalling require the kernel to remember every connection, and when <code>nf_conntrack_max</code> is reached new connections are silently dropped with a kernel log line. Check <code>conntrack -C</code> against the max, and <code>dmesg</code> for 'table full'. Common on nodes with many short-lived connections."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Small API requests work but large POST bodies hang forever on the same path. What is it?", "A path MTU black hole. An overlay network or VPN lowers the effective MTU, a large packet is too big with the don't-fragment bit set, and the router's ICMP fragmentation-needed message is blocked by a firewall — so path MTU discovery never happens and the packet just vanishes. Small requests fit and succeed; large ones do not. Test by lowering the MTU on the interface and seeing whether it clears."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "A Node service degrades over hours and recovers on restart, with a flat heap. What are your hypotheses?", "Descriptor leak (check <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd</code>); Buffer or native addon leak (RSS and <code>external</code> rising, invisible in a heap snapshot); allocator fragmentation, particularly on a many-core machine with many glibc arenas — try <code>MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2</code>; or an unbounded internal queue, which is usually ignored stream backpressure. Take two heap snapshots minutes apart first, and if they match, the problem is outside the heap."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Your database is slow. How do you tell whether it is queries, storage, or contention?", "<code>iostat -x 1</code>: high <code>await</code> with a deep queue means the device is saturated, and <code>wa</code> in <code>top</code> confirms the machine is storage-bound. <code>pg_stat_activity</code>: <code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code> means blocked on another transaction — <code>pg_blocking_pids()</code> names it — and <code>'LWLock'</code> means internal contention. Neither of those and it is query plans, at which point EXPLAIN ANALYZE. And check whether the volume has exhausted its burst credits."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Logs stop just before the crash. Why, and how do you fix it?", "Buffering. Output to a pipe — which is what stdout is in a container — is buffered rather than line-buffered, so the last chunk dies with the process. Also, an abrupt exit skips Node's own async stdout flush. Fixes: flush explicitly before exiting, use a logger that handles it, avoid <code>process.exit()</code> in favour of letting the loop drain, and remember that OOM kills give you nothing at all because SIGKILL cannot be handled."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "A pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. Walk through your diagnosis.", "<code>kubectl logs --previous</code> for the dead container's output, then the exit code, which tells you the class: 137 is SIGKILL, almost always OOM — confirm with <code>lastState.terminated.reason</code>; 139 is a segmentation fault, so a native addon; 127 is command-not-found, so a wrong entrypoint path or a missing shared library, which on Alpine usually means a glibc binary; 1 is your application throwing. Only then look at the application."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "After a restart your server fails to bind with EADDRINUSE for about a minute. Why?", "Sockets from the previous process are in TIME_WAIT, which lasts 60 seconds on Linux — it exists so a delayed duplicate packet cannot be delivered to a new connection with the same four-tuple, and so a lost final ACK can still be answered. <code>SO_REUSEADDR</code> allows binding anyway, and every server framework sets it, so if you see this you are probably on a raw socket."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "You get intermittent ECONNRESET from a service you call, only under load. What is the likely cause?", "A keep-alive race: your agent picks a pooled socket at the same instant the server closes it for idleness. Set your client's idle timeout below the server's, and retry idempotent requests on ECONNRESET. If it is not that, it is a load balancer idle timeout in the middle, or the server closing with unread data in its receive buffer."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Your CI build is fast locally and slow in Docker on a Mac. Why?", "Bind mounts cross the boundary into the Linux VM through a filesystem protocol, and every <code>stat</code> pays it — which a bundler or test runner does a hundred thousand times over <code>node_modules</code>. Keep <code>node_modules</code> in a named volume rather than bind-mounted, or run the whole workflow inside the container. On Windows the equivalent is working inside the WSL2 filesystem rather than <code>/mnt/c</code>."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "You deleted a huge log file and df still shows the disk full. What now?", "The link count reached zero but a running process still has it open, so the data is not freed. <code>lsof | grep deleted</code> finds the holder; restarting or signalling it releases the space. Next time truncate the file (<code>: > file</code>) instead of deleting it, or configure log rotation with copytruncate."),
("recovery", "Recovery scenario", "Your app works on your machine and fails in CI with 'Cannot find module ./Button'. Why?", "Case sensitivity. APFS on macOS is case-insensitive by default and ext4 in the Linux container is not, so <code>./button</code> resolving to <code>Button.tsx</code> works locally and fails in CI. It is a filesystem property, not a bundler bug, and a lint rule or a case-sensitive volume catches it earlier."),
]
